name: disks
boss: coroner
flavor: A drive that might already be dead, and the tools that find out.
brief: `pspy` watches every process that starts, and `strace` follows one
brief: program's system calls -- the requests it makes to the kernel, like
brief: opening a file. You meet both here, against a live fixture.
brief: `ddrescue` copies a failing source once, carefully, logging what it
brief: could not read; that also runs here, against a small file standing
brief: in for a dying disk. `testdisk`, `photorec` and `smartctl` are
brief: full-screen or read real hardware, so on the stick they run through
brief: `xexec`; here you are graded on knowing the right invocation, not
brief: on running it.
tools: pspy strace ddrescue testdisk photorec smartctl
---
q: {watch every new process for exactly 3 seconds|capture 3 seconds of every process that starts|keep an eye on process starts for a 3-second window}, with `pspy`
a: timeout 3 pspy 2>/dev/null || true
out: [Pp]spy
ref: pspy
teach: pspy
hint: timeout caps the watch at 3s, pspy needs no root, `|| true` swallows a nonzero exit
---
q: {see which files it tries to open|watch its open-related syscalls happen|catch exactly what it opens, syscall by syscall} -- trace every `open`/`openat` call `busybox true` makes, first 8 lines, with `strace`
a: strace -f -e trace=open,openat busybox true 2>&1 | head -8
out: exited with 0
ref: strace
teach: strace
hint: -f follows forks, -e trace= filters to the syscalls named, 2>&1 carries strace's own stderr into the pipe
---
q: {the source might be failing, so copy what you still can|treat it like a dying drive and pull off every readable byte|assume part of it won't read, and salvage what does} -- rescue ``mystery.bin`` into ``rescued.img``, logging the bad spots to ``rescue.map``, with `ddrescue`
a: ddrescue -f mystery.bin rescued.img rescue.map 2>&1 | tail -4
out: Finished
ref: ddrescue
teach: ddrescue
hint: -f forces the output file, then source, destination image, then the map that survives a retry
seed: dd if=/dev/zero of=mystery.bin bs=1k count=4 2>/dev/null
---
q: ``image.img`` {lost its partition table -- rebuild it|has a wiped partition table -- put it back|came back with no partition table at all -- reconstruct it}, with `testdisk`
a: testdisk image.img
ref: testdisk
teach: testdisk
hint: one argument, the image -- full-screen after that, so this is graded on the invocation alone
---
q: ``image.img`` {has no usable partition table -- carve files out by their shape instead|partition table is gone -- recover files by their raw shape instead|table is unreadable -- pull files straight out by matching their shape}, with `photorec`
a: photorec image.img
ref: photorec
teach: photorec
hint: same one argument as testdisk -- the last resort when the table itself is unrecoverable
---
q: ``/dev/sda`` {might be failing -- get its health verdict|could be on its way out -- ask for a pass/fail health check|may be dying -- pull its overall health status}, with `smartctl`
a: smartctl -H /dev/sda
ref: smartctl
teach: smartctl
hint: -H asks for the overall pass/fail health verdict, before you trust or wipe the drive
